\title{Fork-Pinning: An Information-Theoretic Criterion for Congruence Determination of Prime Splitting}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{14 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $f \in \mathbb{Z}[x]$ be monic and separable, and let a \emph{fork} be any binary-valued function of the splitting type of $f$ modulo a prime $p$. We ask when such a fork is determined by a congruence condition on $p$ and, when it is not, exactly how much information a congruence still carries. Working in the Chebotarev model, where the Frobenius element of the Galois group $G$ of the splitting field is uniform on $G$ and congruence data corresponds to abelian characters of $G$, we prove the \emph{fork-pinning criterion}: a fork $Y$ satisfies $I(X;Y) = H(Y)$ for some abelian character $X$ if and only if $Y$ factors through the abelianization $G^{\ab} = G/[G,G]$, equivalently if and only if $Y(gc) = Y(g)$ for every commutator $c$. The abelianization is the optimal congruence observable, and equality of information for all forks holds precisely when a character's kernel equals $[G,G]$. We compute exact values on three Galois groups, matching measurement over thousands of primes: a cyclic cubic is pinned at $100\%$ of its entropy with $I = H = \log 3 - \tfrac23\log 2 = 0.9183$ bits; an $S_3$ cubic yields only the sign, $I = \tfrac43\log2 + \tfrac12\log3 - \tfrac56\log5 = 0.1909$ bits, with deficit exactly $\tfrac12 H(1/3)$; an $S_4$ quartic yields $I = \tfrac32\log2 - \tfrac58\log 5 = 0.0488$ bits for the has-a-root fork, and every within-face fork is exactly flat. Perfect Galois groups such as $A_5$ are congruence-blind. We determine the capacity of a binary fork ($I \le \log 2$, attained iff the observable determines the fork and the fork is balanced; for $S_n$ attained by exactly the sign and its negation), establish the sharp extremal profile $\Phi(d) = h(d) - \tfrac12h(2d)$ of an index-two conductor together with its monotonicity and small-$d$ asymptotics, and quantify the semiprime barrier: the information at the level of $N = pq$ is a universal function $D(n)$ of the group order with $D(n) \le \frac{1}{(2n-1)(n-1)}$, $n^2D(n) < 1$, and $n^2D(n) \to 1 - \log 2$, while the which-factor channel has capacity exactly zero, for two factors and for $k$.
\end{abstract}

